function renderGuide(){$('guide').innerHTML=`<article class="card"><h2>Grown-up guide</h2><div class="row noPrint"><button id="printGuide">Print teaching guide</button><button id="backCourse">Back to course</button></div><p>Eleven core episodes and one optional extension, for short sessions together. There is no required pace. Start with equality and reach simple equations early. Counters, drawings, spoken explanations and symbols are all welcome.</p><h3>A short entry conversation</h3><p>Is 5 = 3 + 2 true? (Yes.) What belongs in 3 + 4 = □ + 5? (2.) Hide three of seven counters: how many are visible? (4.) Ask why before interpreting an error. Before equal groups, share eight into two equal groups (4 each).</p><h3>Connect school methods to algebra</h3><p>The first six episodes show the same problem with a missing box or part–whole bar and with a letter. Let the child identify the whole and parts, then match each picture action to an equation step. These are common box and bar representations, not a claim that every Singapore Math textbook teaches identically. Use the picture as a bridge; a child who explains the equation need not draw a bar every time.</p><h3>How to teach</h3><p>Predict a step, reveal it, explain it, then try a new example. Add the same amount to both sides; distinguish this from reordering an addition. Use the whole side when multiplying or dividing. Choose an inverse operation, rather than saying “move it and change its sign.” A legal move need not be the most useful move.</p><p>Episode 6 has a pause between understanding 2x and dividing groups. Episode 10 has a pause between solving with bars and replacing a letter with an equal expression. Before episode 10, revisit two-step equations, known-number substitution, and comparison language.</p><h3>Pause, revisit, or move on?</h3><p>After a few problems, ask the child to explain why a step keeps the equality true and check an answer in the original problem. If that needs help, keep the model and revisit the same idea another day. If the child can explain a new example, try the next episode. At a later session, use “Try a later check” on an earlier episode; hints remain available and help is recorded. This is a conversation, not a timed test. The fixed checkpoint and its answers below remain available too.</p><h3>Progress and privacy</h3><p>Profiles save separately in this browser. The earlier eight-lesson counts remain in a legacy record; they are not treated as mastery. In-page hints, revealed steps, and corrections count as supported practice. “Without an in-page hint” does not prove independence: an adult may have helped. Use the observation selector after hearing a fresh explanation. Nothing is locked.</p><h3>Algebra notes</h3><p>Each letter holds one value within a problem. Different letters may hold the same value. 2x means two times x, not x + 2. A and C are counts, not names of people. Matching C bars represent equal amounts, not two actual groups of children. Division by zero is undefined; multiplying by zero hides information. These exceptions do not need to lead the first lesson.</p><h3>Standalone materials</h3><p>This HTML includes all teaching explanations, activities, practice, and checkpoint answers. It needs no internet. The companion 200-page coursebook has matching color and monochrome editions, detailed teaching, two worked examples per episode, writing space, mixed practice, reference pages, and a full answer key.</p><h3>Reset this explorer</h3><p>Reset only ${state.person}'s progress, including the preserved old counts.</p><button id="resetProgress">Reset this explorer…</button><div id="resetConfirm" hidden><p>This will erase this explorer’s saved place, observations, and counts. It cannot be undone.</p><button id="confirmReset">Yes, erase this explorer’s progress</button><button id="cancelReset">Keep my progress</button></div><p id="resetStatus" role="status"></p></article>${lessons.map((l,i)=>`<article class="card"><div class="eyebrow">${l.tag}</div><h2>${l.title}</h2><p>${l.goal}</p><p>${l.text}</p><div class="math">${l.start}</div><ol>${l.steps.map(([eq,why])=>`<li><strong>${eq}</strong> — ${why}</li>`).join('')}</ol><p>${l.note}</p><p><strong>Discuss:</strong> ${l.ask}</p><p><strong>Checkpoint:</strong> ${l.check.q}</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> ${l.check.answers.join(', ')}. ${l.check.hint}</p>${bridgeHTML(l)}</article>`).join('')}`;$('printGuide').onclick=()=>window.print();$('backCourse').onclick=toggleGuide;$('resetProgress').onclick=()=>{$('resetConfirm').hidden=false};$('cancelReset').onclick=()=>{$('resetConfirm').hidden=true};$('confirmReset').onclick=()=>{$('resetConfirm').hidden=true;state.profiles[state.person]=blankProfile();save();$('resetStatus').textContent='Progress reset. Return to the course to start again.';chapter=0;nav()}}
function toggleGuide(){guidedOpen=!guidedOpen;$('lesson').classList.toggle('hidden',guidedOpen);$('guide').style.display=guidedOpen?'block':'none';$('robotBuddy').classList.toggle('hidden',guidedOpen);$('guideToggle').textContent=guidedOpen?'Back to course':'Grown-up guide';if(guidedOpen)renderGuide()}
